"Moving St. Peter's obelisk as seen in the engravings of Giovanni Guerra and Natale Bonifacio: a technological feat, or pure propaganda? (https://iris.uniroma1.it/handle/11573/481954)\nciteturn149search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; \nSi tratta di illustrazioni che hanno suscitato grande ammirazione, Certamente meno interesse specifico; infatti, esse attendono ancora una verifica contestuale nel dibattito, allora ancora embrionale, tra meccanica teorica e pratica. Inoltre ci si chiede se le informazioni in esse contenute siano sufficienti per trasmettere lo specialissimo “sapere”, qual’è quello dello spostamento di grandi monoliti. Il presente contributo intende riflettere sul portato tecnico e sull’efficacia divulgativa di questa iconografia.\n\nAnno di pubblicazione\n\n2012\n\nTitolo del volume\n\nNuts&Bolts of Construction History. Culture, Tecnology and Society\n\nISBN\n\n9782708409293\n\nParole chiave\n\nSt. Peter's obelisk; obelisk; S. Peter; Domenico Fontana\n\nTipologia\n\n02 Pubblicazione su volume::02a Capitolo o Articolo\n\nCitazione\n\nMoving St. Peter's obelisk as seen in the engravings of Giovanni Guerra and Natale Bonifacio: a technological feat, or pure propaganda? / D'Amelio, Maria Grazia; DE CESARIS, Fabrizio. - STAMPA. - 1(2012), pp. 611-620.\n\nFile allegati a questo prodotto\n\nFile\n---\nDeCesaris_Moving-St-Peter's_2012.pdf solo gestori archivio Tipologia: Documento in Post-print (versione successiva alla peer review e accettata per la pubblicazione) Licenza: Tutti i diritti riservati (All rights reserved) Dimensione 7.03 MB Formato Adobe PDF   Contatta l'autore\n\nI documenti in IRIS sono protetti da copyright e tutti i diritti sono riservati, salvo diversa indicazione.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTEST ON TILE VAULTS IN FRANCE IN THE 19TH CENTURY | Documentos - Universidad Europea de Madrid (https://portalcientifico.universidadeuropea.com/documentos/620a244299a2203d59cd2921?lang=es)\nciteturn149search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.3 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; \n\n# TEST ON TILE VAULTS IN FRANCE IN THE 19TH CENTURY\n\n  1. Esther Redondo Martínez ^{1}\n\n  1. 1 Universidad Europea de Madrid\n\nActas:\n\n4th International Congress on Construction History\n\nEditorial: Picard\n\nISBN: 9782708409293\n\nAño de publicación: 2012\n\nPáginas: 107-115\n\nCongreso: Fourth International Congress on Construction History, Paris, 3-7 July 2012\n\nTipo: Aportación a congreso\n\n  * ## Resumen\n\nDurante el s. XIX las bóvedas tabicadas se desarrollan enormemente, ampliando sus usos a nuevos tipos y extendiendose por zonas dónde no se habían utilizado tradicionalmente. Además, comienza a utilizarse el cemento como aglomerante en lugar del yeso. En este contexto se realizan muchos ensayos sobre ellas, con el objeto de validar un sistema que resultaba nuevo por estas razones. Se estudian a continuación una serie de ensayos de resistencia realizados en Francia entre 1837 y 1865, todos ellos sobre bóvedas de tamaño y geometría similar: entre 4 y 5 m de luz, y con flecha 1/10 de la luz, un tipo muy empleado en ese momento para la construcción de fábricas. El primero de ellos busca medir experimentalmente el empuje de una de estas bóvedas, para cerrar un debate sobre la existencia o no de empujes en las bóvedas tabicadas. Los siguientes quieren obtener la carga de rotura de las bóvedas, con el objeto de construir después unas similares.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTEST ON TILE VAULTS IN FRANCE IN THE 19TH CENTURY | Documents - Universidad Europea de Madrid (https://portalcientifico.universidadeuropea.com/documentos/620a244299a2203d59cd2921)\nciteturn149search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; \n\n# TEST ON TILE VAULTS IN FRANCE IN THE 19TH CENTURY\n\n  1. Esther Redondo Martínez ^{1}\n\n  1. 1 Universidad Europea de Madrid\n\ninfo\n\nUniversidad Europea de Madrid\n\nMadrid, España\n\nROR https://ror.org/04dp46240\n\nImage: Geographic location of the organization Universidad Europea de Madrid\n\nProceedings:\n\n4th International Congress on Construction History\n\nPublisher: Picard\n\nISBN: 9782708409293\n\nYear of publication: 2012\n\nPages: 107-115\n\nCongress: Fourth International Congress on Construction History, Paris, 3-7 July 2012\n\nType: Conference Paper\n\n  * ## Abstract\n\nDurante el s. XIX las bóvedas tabicadas se desarrollan enormemente, ampliando sus usos a nuevos tipos y extendiendose por zonas dónde no se habían utilizado tradicionalmente. Además, comienza a utilizarse el cemento como aglomerante en lugar del yeso. En este contexto se realizan muchos ensayos sobre ellas, con el objeto de validar un sistema que resultaba nuevo por estas razones. Se estudian a continuación una serie de ensayos de resistencia realizados en Francia entre 1837 y 1865, todos ellos sobre bóvedas de tamaño y geometría similar: entre 4 y 5 m de luz, y con flecha 1/10 de la luz, un tipo muy empleado en ese momento para la construcción de fábricas. El primero de ellos busca medir experimentalmente el empuje de una de estas bóvedas, para cerrar un debate sobre la existencia o no de empujes en las bóvedas tabicadas. Los siguientes quieren obtener la carga de rotura de las bóvedas, con el objeto de construir después unas similares.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA mining engineer in heritage land : A.L.W.E. Van der Veen and early research on natural stone for the Netherlands State Commission on Conservation [1920-1936] - TU Delft Research Portal (https://research.tudelft.nl/en/publications/a-mining-engineer-in-heritage-land-alwe-van-der-veen-and-early-re/)\nciteturn149search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; \n\n# A mining engineer in heritage land : A.L.W.E. Van der Veen and early research on natural stone for the Netherlands State Commission on Conservation [1920-1936]\n\n  * WJ Quist\n  * , TG Nijland\n\nResearch output: Chapter in Book/Conference proceedings/Edited volume › Chapter › Scientific\n\nOriginal language  | Undefined/Unknown\nTitle of host publication  | Nuts & bolts of construction history : Culture, technology and society. Vol. 2 : People & organizations / materials\nEditors  | R Carvais, A Guillerme, V Negre, J Sakarovitch\nPlace of Publication  | s.l.\nPublisher  | Picard\nPages  | 435-441\nNumber of pages  | 664\nISBN (Print)  | 9782708409293\nPublication status  | Published - 2012\n\nQuist, WJ., & Nijland, TG. (2012). A mining engineer in heritage land : A.L.W.E. Van der Veen and early research on natural stone for the Netherlands State Commission on Conservation [1920-1936]. In R. Carvais, A. Guillerme, V. Negre, & J. Sakarovitch (Eds.), Nuts & bolts of construction history : Culture, technology and society. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe scientification of reinforced concrete in Belgium during the interwar period: development and dissemination of scientific, theoretical and technical knowledge (https://biblio.ugent.be/publication/2966184)\nciteturn149search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n      booktitle    = {{Nuts & bolts of construction history : Culture, Technology and Society}},\n      editor       = {{Carvais, Robert and Guillerme, André and Nègre, Valérie and Sakarovitch, Joël}},\n      isbn         = {{9782708409293}},\n      language     = {{eng}},\n      location     = {{Paris, France}},\n      pages        = {{637--645}},\n      publisher    = {{Picard}},\n      title        = {{The scientification of reinforced concrete in Belgium during the interwar period: development and dissemination of scientific, theoretical and technical knowledge}},\n      volume       = {{2}},\n      year         = {{2012}},\n    }\n\nSubject\n    Technology and Engineering\nYear\n    2012\nPublication type\n    Conference Paper\nPublication status\n    published\nIn\n    Nuts & bolts of construction history : Culture, Technology and Society\nEditor\n    Robert Carvais, André Guillerme, Valérie Nègre and Joël Sakarovitch\nISBN\n    9782708409293\nVolume\n    2\nPages\n    637 - 645\nPublisher\n    Picard\nPlace of publication\n    Paris, France\nConference name\n    4th International Congress on Construction History\nConference location\n    Paris, France\nConference start\n    2012-07-03\nConference end\n    2012-07-07\nLanguage\n    English\nUGent publication?\n    yes\nUGent classification\n    C1\nCopyright statement\n    No license (in copyright)\nid\n    2966184\nHandle\n    http://hdl.handle.net/1854/LU-2966184\nDate created\n    2012-08-01 09:54:28\nDate last changed\n    2026-03-31 14:30:06--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConstruction Methods in Carolingian Rome (8th-9th Cent.) (https://iris.uniroma1.it/handle/11573/465195)\nciteturn149search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \nThe attempt to imitate the techniques of classical antiquity is evident, as the extremely rare concrete vaulted constructions also testify. The norm in these structures, which differentiate them from the Roman ones, is in fact the irregularity of the laying: the brick courses are undulating; the surfaces are not flat but contain recesses and protrusions; and the joints are not uniformly finished on any one surface. If the uniformity of these constructions is the result of an uninterrupted activity and consistency of programmes permitted by the succession of popes all belonging to the Roman aristocracy, the technical characteristics are only partly justified by the use of non-homogeneous material as a result of their recovery or of hurried workmanship or even of a presumed loss of technical capacity, not apparent in other fields. Among the principal causes one must also consider the possible employment of unskilled labour: some works are known to have been carried out through public tender and also using forced labour, made possible by the political and economic structure of the Papal State.\n\nAnno di pubblicazione\n\n2012\n\nTitolo del volume\n\nNuts and Bolts of Construction History. Culture, Technology and Society\n\nISBN\n\n9782708409293\n\nParole chiave\n\nMedioevo; murature; Roma; epoca carolingia\n\nTipologia\n\n02 Pubblicazione su volume::02a Capitolo o Articolo\n\nCitazione\n\nConstruction Methods in Carolingian Rome (8th-9th Cent.) / Barelli, Lia. - STAMPA. - 2(2012), pp. 135-141.\n\nAppartiene alla tipologia:\n\n02a Capitolo o Articolo\n\nFile allegati a questo prodotto\n\nNon ci sono file associati a questo prodotto.\n\nUtilizza questo identificativo per citare o creare un link a questo documento: `https://hdl.handle.net/11573/465195`\n\nAttenzione! I dati visualizzati non sono stati sottoposti a validazione da parte dell'ateneo--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Cloaca maxima in the roman town of Privernum, Italy: the project, the plan, the construction: (https://airus.unisalento.it/handle/11587/366090)\nciteturn149search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n\n##### 2012-01-01\n\n#### Abstract\n\nThe recent excavation of a Roman republican town, Privernum, 90 km south of Rome, Italy, has allowed the discovery and the investigation of a channel more than 4 meters wide, with vaulted roofing, preserved for about 200 meters. This waterway, surely the result of straightening of a former stream, represents the main sewer of the roman settlement, dating back to the middle republican period; the intention is to excavate and restore it so that it can be used to drain the archaeological area. The intervention, still in progress, has made possible an accurate examination of the building technique and construction process of the channel. It reveals a design integrated directly into the central part of the town; the need to fix exactly the relation between the channel and the urban planning, which was clearly affected by the layout and the orientation of the conduit, suggests the existence of a master plan.\n\nAnno\n\n2012\n\nISBN\n\n9782708409293\n\nAppare nelle tipologie:\n\nRelazione di atto di convegno in volume\n\nFile in questo prodotto:\n\nNon ci sono file associati a questo prodotto.\n\n#####  Informazioni\n\nUtilizza questo identificativo per citare o creare un link a questo documento: `https://hdl.handle.net/11587/366090`--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFor an History of Deconstruction (https://iris.uniroma1.it/handle/11573/466981)\nciteturn149search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; \nAnno di pubblicazione\n\n2012\n\nTitolo del volume\n\nNuts and Bolts of Construction History. Culture, Technology and Society\n\nISBN\n\n9782708409293\n\nAbstract\n\nNel medioevo e in età moderna la demolizione di un edificio era un evento che spesso avviava nuove fasi e trasformazioni dell’oggetto demolito. Questo giustifica l’espressione “Chasteuz abatuz est demi refez”: nuove realtà aspettavano di prodursi a seguito di una demolizione (distruzione o smontaggio) e il recupero e il riuso delle strutture demolite è parte della nuova costruzione. Le azioni e le procedure secondo le quali avveniva la demolizione erano determinate proprio dalla finalità ultima alla quale era volta l’azione di demolizione. Inoltre anche i tempi erano diversi a seconda del caso. Poteva essere immediata e in unica fase o viceversa prodursi nel tempo. La demolizione degli edifici, intesa come l’azione generale distinta in distruzione e smontaggio parziale e totale, è stata eseguita, nei secoli e prima dell’uso degli esplosivi, solo con procedimenti propri del taglio delle pietre. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBéton Armé in the Sinking 